architecture-decision-record:
  identifier: "shlita_05"
  title: A signal that cannot be read keeps a defined value and gains a bad quality, and the reaction to that is declared per module
  status: accepted
  date: "2026-08-30"
  deciders:
    - felix
  superseded-by: ""
  open-questions:
    - How the per-module diagnostic bits are addressed from within a program -- a reserved area of the image, or named system variables -- is left to the crate that lays out the image.
    - Whether a program reads a signal's quality through a function, an accessor on the variable, or a parallel variable it declares itself is a question for ADR shlita_03's dialect rather than for the runtime.
  context: |
    ADR shlita_04 settled who owns the image and when it is filled, and it
    ruled that a failure during `read` leaves the affected values stale, marks
    the driver faulted and lets `process` run anyway. It then named the cost
    of that ruling in its own negatives: a stale image looks exactly like a
    healthy image that is not changing, and the fault bit is the only thing
    that distinguishes them. It did not say what that bit is, where it lives,
    or what a program is supposed to do about it.

    The question that forces all of the rest is whether a signal is required
    for processing at all. Without an answer, a controller has only two
    behaviours available and both are wrong. It can stop on any fault, which
    makes an engine embedded in a tool hostage to the least reliable thing
    attached to it -- one flaky broker and nothing runs. Or it can continue
    through every fault, which means a lost input reads as whatever was there
    an hour ago and the program cannot tell.

    IEC 61131-3 does not answer this, because it assumes terminals that are
    physically present and a scan that samples them. The question only arises
    once the wire can be a network. The fieldbus profiles do answer it, and
    they have answered it for as long as remote I/O has existed: a signal that
    cannot be read takes a substitute value, and it carries a status that says
    the reading is not trustworthy. Profinet and Profibus call the first one
    the substitute value; OPC DA's quality byte and OPC UA's StatusCode are
    the second. This is ADR sheni_10's habit again -- where a standard already
    answers a question, the answer is looked up rather than decided.

    One neighbouring answer in this workspace cannot be borrowed. Sheni's soft
    types put the unknown inside the value space, and `soft_bool` is
    three-valued for exactly that reason. But ADR shlita_01 refused to reuse
    sheni's primitives and kept IEC's BOOL two-valued, so widening a value
    here to carry its own uncertainty would undo that decision rather than
    apply it.
  decision: |
    A signal is one of three things with respect to processing, and the tag
    map says which.

    It is *required* when a POU reads it and cannot produce a meaningful
    output without a real reading. It is *optional* when a POU reads it but a
    defined stand-in is acceptable. It is *unused* when the tag map declares
    it and no POU reads it at all, which is an ordinary condition rather than
    a mistake -- a signal may be logged, forwarded or simply reserved.

    An optional signal must declare a substitute value. This is not a
    convenience and it is not defaultable: a process image has no null, the
    program will read something, and so that something has to be decided
    before the run rather than discovered during it. The substitute takes one
    of the three forms the fieldbus profiles already use, and no fourth is
    invented -- zero, hold the last good value, or a configured constant. Hold
    is not the safe choice it looks like, which is why it is spelled rather
    than assumed.

    Quality sits beside the value, not inside it. Every signal in the image
    has a status alongside it, taking OPC UA's three levels: good, uncertain,
    bad. A substitute value is therefore honest -- it is a real value the
    program can compute with, marked as not having come from the wire -- where
    a value that quietly stood in for a missing reading would be
    indistinguishable from a true one. Keeping quality in a parallel plane is
    also what preserves ADR shlita_01: BOOL stays two-valued, the elementary
    types are untouched, and nothing in the type vocabulary has to learn about
    faults.

    Requiredness is checked against the loaded program rather than merely
    believed. The engine knows which addresses the POUs read, so at load it
    can compare the declaration with the use and turn three cases into
    diagnostics: a signal declared required that nothing reads is a stale
    declaration and warns; a signal declared optional that a POU reads with no
    substitute value declared is an error and refuses to load; a signal
    declared unused that a POU reads is an error for the same reason, because
    it has no substitute either. Configuration mistakes that would otherwise
    surface as a wrong number at three in the morning become load-time errors
    with codes.

    The reaction to a fault is declared per module, not once for the engine.
    Real controllers configure fault reaction per device rather than globally,
    and the reason is that the modules genuinely differ: the loss of a safety
    interlock and the loss of a temperature trend are not the same event. Three
    policies. Under `stop`, the engine enters a fault state, outputs are driven
    to their configured safe state, and `process` refuses to run until the
    fault is acknowledged. Under `substitute`, the substitute values are
    applied, the affected signals go to bad quality, and the scan continues.
    Under `ignore`, the fault is recorded in diagnostics and nothing else
    changes.

    The policy field is mandatory and has no default. Both available defaults
    are dangerous and they are dangerous in opposite directions, so neither
    can be the quiet one: defaulting to `stop` makes an embedded engine
    hostage to its least reliable transport, and defaulting to `substitute`
    lets a lost interlock read as zero forever without anyone having chosen
    that. ADR sheni_02 refused to let a duration sniff which convention it had
    been handed and turned three conventions into three types; this is the
    same refusal, and the cost is one field written once per module.

    `diagnostics` is a read-only view. It reports, per driver, the driver's
    state, the last error code, the count of consecutive failures and the time
    of the last good read; and for the engine, its state, the scan count, the
    last scan duration and a bounded ring of recent events. The ring is the
    diagnostic buffer that every real CPU carries, and it is the single most
    useful thing available when a fault is intermittent, because the fault is
    gone by the time anybody looks.

    What `diagnostics` deliberately does not do is decide. The policy decides,
    `read` enforces, `diagnostics` reports, and the quality plane is how the
    program itself sees the same event. Making the query the decision point
    would put control flow behind a call the host has to remember to make
    between `read` and `process`, and a safety property that depends on
    someone remembering to ask a question is not a property.

    Diagnostic status is also mapped into the image as bits, the way a real
    CPU maps rack and module health, so a program can read whether a module is
    healthy without a round trip through the host. A controller that can only
    learn about its own faults by being told from outside cannot be the thing
    that reacts to them.

    Fault, substitute and quality errors take the 750-769 sub-band ADR
    shlita_01 reserved for the scan runtime.
  consequences:
    positive:
      - A stale value and a fresh one are distinguishable, which is the defect ADR shlita_04 recorded against itself, closed.
      - The unknown is carried beside the value rather than inside it, so the elementary types stay exactly as ADR shlita_01 defined them and no function has to learn a third case.
      - A program can react to a degraded input in its own logic, rather than the fault being visible only to the host that embeds it.
      - Comparing declared requiredness against actual use turns a class of configuration mistakes into load-time errors instead of runtime surprises.
      - Per-module policy lets a lost interlock and a lost temperature trend have different consequences, which is the distinction that makes a fault policy worth having at all.
      - Substitute value and quality are looked up from the fieldbus profiles rather than invented, so an engineer who has configured remote I/O before already knows what the fields mean.
      - A bounded diagnostic ring makes intermittent faults investigable after they have stopped happening.
    negative:
      - Every signal in the image now costs a status beside its value, which is memory spent on every scan whether or not anything is faulted.
      - A mandatory policy field with no default is friction on every module, and the first reaction to friction is to write the same value everywhere without thinking about it.
      - Three signal states, three substitute forms and three policies is a configuration surface with twenty-seven combinations, not all of which are sensible, and nothing but review prevents the silly ones.
      - Hold-last as a substitute form re-creates precisely the ambiguity this record set out to remove, and only the quality bit distinguishes it -- a program that ignores quality is exactly as badly off as before.
      - Mapping diagnostics into the image means the image layout carries a reserved region, and reserved regions are how address spaces become hard to change later.
      - The load-time cross-check needs to know which addresses a POU reads, which is a static analysis of the program, and it will be wrong in the direction of over-reporting on anything computed at runtime.
  alternatives-rejected:
    -
      name: One fault policy for the whole engine
      reason: It forces the loss of an interlock and the loss of a trend to have the same consequence. Whichever setting is chosen is wrong for half the modules, and the workaround is to split the program across engines, which is a worse configuration surface than one field.
    -
      name: Let an optional signal simply be absent, and read as null
      reason: A process image has no null and cannot acquire one without every function in ADR shlita_01's standard library growing a third case. The program will read something regardless, so the only question is whether that value is chosen deliberately or by accident.
    -
      name: Carry the uncertainty in the value, reusing sheni's soft types
      reason: It reverses ADR shlita_01, which kept IEC's two-valued BOOL rather than sheni's three-valued soft twin. The soft types answer a different question -- data that was never known -- where this is data that is known but not currently readable, and conflating them would make every arithmetic function ask about provenance.
    -
      name: A single fault flag per driver, with no per-signal quality
      reason: A driver commonly carries dozens of signals and fails for one of them, so a flag at driver granularity is either over-broad or wrong. It also gives the program nothing to branch on at the point where it actually uses the value.
    -
      name: Let `diagnostics` decide whether the cycle proceeds
      reason: It puts control flow behind a query the host must remember to make between `read` and `process`. A fault reaction that depends on someone asking about it is not a reaction, and it makes a read-only view into a state transition.
    -
      name: Derive requiredness entirely from use, with nothing declared
      reason: Use says whether a signal is read, not whether a stand-in is acceptable for it. Those are different facts and only the second one is a judgement about the process. Deriving both would mean every read signal is required, which is the global policy this record rejected.
    -
      name: Default the module policy to `stop`, as the conservative choice
      reason: It is conservative for a controller wired to a plant and reckless for an engine embedded in a tool, where the least reliable transport attached to it would then be able to halt everything. Conservative is not a property of a setting on its own; it depends on what is on the other end, which is exactly what the field exists to record.
